---
// /town/ — THE CIVIC HUB, and the town's own civic face.
//
// THE FOUNDER'S RULING, 2026-08-30: the Town page absorbs Stamps entirely and
// is restructured around the civic quarter — five buildings you click to open
// the lane inside. /stamps/ is now a forwarder, and every fragment it ever
// answered to still lands (see town/pages/stamps/index.astro).
//
// WHY THE TOWN PAGE AND NOT A NEW ROUTE. /town/ already existed as "the town's
// own bare read" — the aggregate a reader gets when they click The Town — and
// it carried one breath of prose and nothing else. That is the page this ask
// describes; it did not need a sibling, it needed a body. The old page's head
// survives verbatim below, and its foot with it. Nothing that stood here was
// dropped to make room.
//
// ── THE ONTOLOGY IS THE WORLD'S, NOT THIS PAGE'S ─────────────────────────────
// The three asks are constitutional marks, and the sentence is quoted rather
// than paraphrased (`the-town/the-three-asks`):
//
//   "Quests are the town's asks for its residents; bounties are residents' asks
//    for other residents; blueprints are residents' asks for the town."
//
// Five lanes stand on that; src/lib/civic.mjs owns the list and this page
// renders it. Two of the five buildings DO NOT STAND in the pinned world yet
// (the Quest Guild and the Marketplace, and on this pin the Think Tank too),
// and the page says so on the building itself rather than drawing a door onto
// nothing. Which are standing is READ from the world store, never written down
// here — so a building lights up the day its mark lands, with no edit.
//
// ── STAMPS IS MACHINERY, NOT A BUILDING ──────────────────────────────────────
// Stamps-the-currency is the blood of every lane above, so it is a rules
// section under the quarter rather than a sixth house. Everything the portal
// carried is here: the market's two blocks, the dials, and the nine teaching
// accordions, all MOVED — not rewritten, not forked. The prose is byte-identical
// to what /stamps/ served, which is why the founder-reviewed sentences and the
// content laws in test/civic-hub.test.mjs still say the same thing.
//
// ── THE SCROLL OF DOOM, AND WHY THERE IS ONE PANEL ───────────────────────────
// The portal was ~900 lines of page. Five lanes concatenated would be a scroll
// nobody reads. That was answered first with five <details> folds shut by
// default, and answered again on 2026-09-01 by the founder: "Instead of stacked
// boxes, we should just have one panel that switches when each building is
// clicked." So the five folds are one region with five children and exactly one
// showing — the quarter above is still the way in, and now it is the ONLY way
// in, which is what makes the buildings mean something.
//
// WITHOUT JAVASCRIPT this page still works, and by a different mechanism than
// before. The buildings are ordinary anchors; the fragment they set is what
// `:target` / `:has(:target)` read; the swap is CSS with no script in it at
// all. The script at the foot only measures the scroll after the swap and runs
// the "?" dialog — see its header. A browser without `:has()` shows every panel
// stacked, which is the page this replaced.
// EVERY IMPORT AT THE TOP, and that is a rule rather than a tidiness. Astro
// hoists frontmatter imports out of the component body, but an import sitting
// after other statements is left where it stands — inside a function, where
// `import` is a syntax error. The build says only `Unexpected "{"` at the
// frontmatter's closing line, which points at neither the import nor the file
// it came from.
import PostmarkLayout from "@/layouts/PostmarkLayout.astro";
// HOLO_LINE / HOLO_NAME_LINE are NOT imported here any more. They were, for the
// pots block's holo paragraph, which moved to /stamps/ on 2026-08-31 — and an
// import kept "just in case" is how a retyped copy of a ruling's sentence finds
// its way back onto a page that is no longer supposed to carry one.
import { livePots, loadPots, POT_FIXTURE } from "@/lib/funding.mjs";
import PotCards from "@/components/PotCards.astro";
import { loadWorldState, notices, FIXTURE, BOARD_PLACE, ASK_MAX } from "@/lib/board.mjs";
// `predicatesOf` and `HOW_IDEAS_ENTER_PLACE` are NOT imported here any more —
// the 2026-09-01 minimalism ruling took both renders off the page (§ rule 1 and
// § rule 2 below). Both still exist in civic.mjs and both are still tested; an
// import kept "just in case" is how a struck block finds its way back, which
// this page has now been taught twice (HOLO_LINE was the first).
import {
  LANES, DEFAULT_LANE, standing, ideas, questStandings, questCards, marketplace,
  readPen, loadPlaceMarks, plaque, markStakes, stakeOf,
  ideaDashboard, boardDashboard, worldPin,
  BLUEPRINTS_REPO, THINK_TANK_PLACE,
  loadBlueprints, blueprintIndex, byStage,
} from "@/lib/civic.mjs";
import { paint, tint, SPRITE_W, SPRITE_H } from "@/lib/civic-art.mjs";
import { tutorialFor } from "@/lib/civic-tutorial.mjs";
import LaneHead from "@/components/LaneHead.astro";
import LaneTutorial from "@/components/LaneTutorial.astro";
import houseRegistry from "@/data/postmark/households.json";
import meeps from "@/data/postmark/meeps.json";
import bulletin from "@/data/postmark/bulletin.json";

const TOWN = "https://github.com/postmark-town/postmark/blob/main";
const WORLD = "https://github.com/keeminlee/postmark-world/blob/main";
// ECONOMY.md moved to the blueprints documentation shelf 2026-08-30; the
// world-root stub still resolves, but citations point at the living page.
const ECON = "https://github.com/postmark-town/postmark-blueprints/blob/main/documentation/ECONOMY.md";
// THE DIALS LEFT WITH THE TEACHING, and `econ` went with them. This page had a
// `readEconomy(loadEconomy())` for the tile dashboard; the dashboard is at
// /stamps/ now, and the pots read their own emission, so nothing here asks the
// ledger anything.

// The card's roll caps at three names — the fourth donation spilled the card
// (founder, 2026-08-29 night). The pot's own /fund/<pot>/ page is the full
// roll, so "and N more" links there rather than truncating anyone away.

// the live half — the same two readers, unchanged
const wantFixture = import.meta.env.DEV &&
  (process.env.SEAM_FIXTURE === "1" || Astro.url.searchParams.has("fixture"));
// THE STORE IS READ ONCE AND SHARED. On the portal this expression loaded the
// world inline, because the board was the only reader on the page. The hub has
// three (the board, the quarter's standing buildings, and the Think Tank's
// ideas), and a second `loadWorldState()` is a second readFileSync + parse of
// the same file for the same answer. The reader's math is board.mjs's and is
// untouched; only the load moved up a line.
const worldState = wantFixture ? FIXTURE : loadWorldState();
const board = notices(worldState);
const openNotices = board.notices.filter((n) => n.status === "open");
const doneNotices = board.notices.filter((n) => n.status === "done");
const potBoard = livePots(wantFixture ? POT_FIXTURE : loadPots());

const usd = (n) => "$" + Number(n).toLocaleString("en-US");
const pct = (p) => Math.round(Math.max(0, Math.min(1, p)) * 100);

// This page's OWN sections, and nothing else. The board and the pots are the
// hub's — listing them here would put two dead fragments at the top of the
// Guide, which is the failure the split invites.



// WHEN THIS DATA WAS MADE, from the emission's own stamp — a quiet market and
// a stale page look identical without it. Every row carries the same value;
// the first that has one speaks for the block.
const potsAsOf = potBoard.pots.map((p) => p.generatedAt).find(Boolean) ?? null;
const asOfText = potsAsOf
  ? new Date(potsAsOf).toISOString().replace("T", " ").slice(0, 16) + " UTC"
  : null;

// ── the civic quarter ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
const declaredHouses = Object.keys(houseRegistry.households ?? {}).length;

// These read the SAME `worldState` the board above read — one load, several
// readers, so the quarter's buildings and the board can never disagree about
// which world this build is rendering.
//
// THE AUTHORED MARKS ARE READ BESIDE IT, and civic.mjs's header says why at
// length: a mark is shipped twice, as somebody's pen under WORLD/marks and as a
// row in the derived world-state.json, and on the pin this page is built
// against those two disagree. The pen is the authoritative one.
// THE PEN IS READ WHOLE NOW, not two levels deep. `readPen` walks the marks
// tree and keys every mark by its own id (`<by>/<slug>`), which is what finally
// reaches the Bounty Board's and the Ballot House's plaques — both filed under
// the town centre rather than at the root, and both silently unreachable to the
// old two-level walk. `placeMarks` is its bodies-only projection, which is the
// shape `standing()` and the marketplace's own line already take.
const pen = readPen();
const placeMarks = loadPlaceMarks();
const quarter = standing(worldState, placeMarks);

// THE ESCROW LEDGER, read once and shared, exactly as the world store is.
// `world-state.json § portfolios` is the one source of a mark's stake — see
// civic.mjs § THE ONE SOURCE OF A MARK'S STAKE for why it is this record and
// not the fold's totals, and for the cross-check that makes it trustworthy.
const stakes = markStakes(worldState);

// THE CHEST CITES THE IDEA (POS-97, 2026-09-15): "drawn" is a join against the
// blueprints chest this build fetched, never a field on the idea mark. A chest
// this build could not read leaves every idea proposed, and says nothing false.
const chest = blueprintIndex(loadBlueprints());
const thinkTank = ideas(worldState, { places: placeMarks, stakes, chest });
const tankStages = byStage(thinkTank.ideas);
const quests = questStandings(bulletin);
const questRows = questCards(quests);
const market = marketplace(bulletin);

// THE FIVE PLAQUES, per lane, read from the record. No sentence below is typed
// into this page; the falsifier in test/civic-hub.test.mjs is what keeps it
// that way.
//
// ── THE PREDICATES ARE NOT READ HERE ANY MORE ────────────────────────────────
// A `PREDICATES` map stood beside this and each panel rendered its rows under
// the plaque: `asked-by · the town`, `pays · town mint — the law stands at
// the-town/quest`, `daily · household read:"quests"`, `pots · darko-fund,
// keeping-ec2`. Every row was true and every row was READ, which is exactly why
// it took a ruling rather than a bug report to remove them: the founder, on
// 2026-09-01, "minimalism minimalism minimalism" — and, as a class, NO MACHINE
// VOICE ON A HUMAN PAGE. Door verbs, mark ids, file names and fund paths are
// the record's law-voice serialized, and they belong at the doors and behind
// the "?" deck (which cites every one of them, file and line).
//
// `predicatesOf` is UNTOUCHED in civic.mjs and still under test. This is a
// render that was removed, not a derivation.
const PLAQUES = Object.fromEntries(LANES.map((l) => [l.key, plaque(l, { pen, state: worldState })]));

// The two dashboards, derived from the same ledger the cards are ordered by —
// so a count under a lane and the order of the cards above it can never
// disagree about who is backing what.
const tankBoard = ideaDashboard(thinkTank, stakes);
const bountyBoard = boardDashboard(board.notices, stakes);

// Each lane's anchor is the id its building points at. The Bounty Board's is
// `board` and not `bounty-board` on purpose: /stamps/#board, /board/, the home
// page's milestone link and every letter ever written point at that word, and
// the block that answers to it is moving house today. A rename would strand
// all of them to save one hyphen.
const LANE_ANCHORS = Object.fromEntries(LANES.map((l) => [l.key, l.id]));
// the lane row itself, by key — every panel hands its OWN row to LaneHead
// rather than restating the lane's name, its who-line or its live flag, so
// there is exactly one place any of those three is written down
const LANE_BY = Object.fromEntries(LANES.map((l) => [l.key, l]));
// LANE_PLACES IS GONE with the last thing that used it. It existed so a lane
// could look its own plaque up by place id; `plaque(lane, …)` takes the lane
// and reads `lane.place` itself, so a second map from key to place was a
// second way to name the same thing.

// EVERY LANE'S TINT, from the same table its building is painted with. Handed
// to each panel as two custom properties rather than written into the stylesheet
// — a hex typed in the <style> block below would be a second palette, and two
// palettes for one lane is exactly one too many.
const LANE_TINTS = Object.fromEntries(LANES.map((l) => [l.key, tint(l.key)]));
const tintVars = (key) => {
  const t = LANE_TINTS[key];
  return `--lane-edge:${t.edge};--lane-edge-open:${t.edgeOpen};--lane-wash:${t.wash};--lane-wash-hover:${t.washHover}`;
};

// THE QUAY NOTE IS NOT READ HERE ANY MORE, and the way it goes matters because
// it is the sentence that bought this page's whole read-never-copy discipline.
//
// It was a hand copy, made four hours before the founder rewrote the mark, and
// the page recited the dead version for a day. The fix was to READ the mark —
// `placeMarks[HOW_IDEAS_ENTER_PLACE]`, rendered under the tank's cards as "How
// an idea enters, in the town's own words on the quay: …". That fix was right
// and it is now redundant: the founder's 2026-09-01 ruling made the Think
// Tank's own PLAQUE the panel's title, and the plaque already says how an idea
// enters. Two marks answering the same question on one panel is the thing being
// removed — "say each thing once", and "the plaque already says it".
//
// THE DISCIPLINE SURVIVES THE REMOVAL and is asserted harder than before: the
// page now holds no copy AND makes no second reading. `HOW_IDEAS_ENTER_PLACE`
// is still exported and still tested in civic.mjs's own suite; the mark still
// stands in the world for renderings that are not this one.

// THE VIGNETTE HAS NO DESCRIPTION, and that is the ruling rather than an
// omission. It carried the town centre's lamplit-quay sentence, which was the
// wrong mark speaking; the fix looked like quoting the quarter's OWN plaque
// instead, and that shipped for one build. The founder walked it and went
// further: with all five buildings drawn and named right there, any prose under
// the heading only says again what the picture already says.
//
//   THE HEADING AND THE FIVE NAMED BUILDINGS ARE THE DESCRIPTION.
//
// So nothing replaces it. `the-town/the-civic-quarter` still carries its plaque
// in the world, for renderings that are not this one.

// `LANE_IDS` IS GONE, and the way it went is worth a line, because for one
// build it was WRONG and harmless at the same time.
//
// It was a literal list of the five PANEL ids that the fold router used to
// decide what it might open. Rewriting it as `LANES.map((l) => l.id)` looked
// like the same list read from the source of truth and is not: the bounty
// lane's `id` is `board` — the INNER block's anchor, which every deep link
// ever written points at — while its panel is `bounty-board`. A lane's anchor
// and a lane's panel are two different ids on purpose, and one of them cannot
// be derived from the other.
//
// It never showed, because the router that consumed it left with the folds and
// nothing has read it since. A wrong value nothing reads is a trap with a
// timer on it, so it goes rather than gets corrected — the panel ids live in
// the markup and in test/civic-hub.test.mjs's own list, which is where a test
// that checks the markup should keep them.

// WHEN THIS WORLD WAS READ — the pin, because the world store carries no clock
// of its own (civic.mjs § WHEN THIS WORLD WAS READ). Short form for a caption;
// the whole sha is on the build stamp for anyone who needs to resolve it.
const pin = worldPin();
const worldAsOf = pin ? `world ${pin.slice(0, 8)}` : null;

// ── THE SWITCH, AS CSS, GENERATED FROM THE LANE LIST ─────────────────────────
// This block is BUILT rather than typed because it needs one rule per lane and
// a hand-written set of five would be five places a lane could be forgotten —
// exactly the "written-down list" failure the buildings themselves are read to
// avoid. It goes out as `is:inline` so Astro leaves the selectors alone; every
// rule is scoped under `main.civic` by hand for the same reason.
//
// NO HEX IS TYPED HERE EITHER. The selected building's colour is
// `--lane-edge-open` / `--lane-wash`, which each `<li class="cq-lot">` carries
// from `tint()` — the same table its sprite is painted from.
//
// ── THE FLOOR, AND WHY IT IS `@supports` ─────────────────────────────────────
// The whole switch rests on `:has()`. A browser without it does not merely lose
// the tint — a selector list containing an unsupported pseudo-class is dropped
// WHOLE, so the naive arrangement leaves `display:none` standing and the reader
// gets no panel at all. So the base rule outside the @supports shows EVERY
// panel, stacked, which is the page this replaced; the switching only exists
// where the browser can actually do it. A degraded reader sees more than they
// asked for, never less.
const switchCss = [
  "main.civic .cq-panels > .c-lane{display:block}",
  "@supports selector(:has(*)){",
  "main.civic .cq-panels > .c-lane{display:none}",
  "main.civic .cq-panels > .c-lane:target{display:block}",
  // the inner ids — #board and #pots are blocks inside a lane, not lanes
  "main.civic .cq-panels > .c-lane:has(:target){display:block}",
  `main.civic .cq-panels:not(:has(:target)) > .c-lane[data-panel="${DEFAULT_LANE}"]{display:block}`,
  // THE SELECTED BUILDING, one rule per lane.
  //
  // IT LIFTS AS WELL AS TINTS, and that was read off the built page rather
  // than designed: with the tint alone, the Think Tank — whose accent is the
  // coldest and lowest-contrast of the five — read as barely selected while
  // the Bounty Board's warm brown read clearly. A selection that depends on
  // which lane you picked is not a selection. The lift is the same 4px the
  // hover already uses, on the same element and for the same reason (the quay
  // band must not come up with it), so a selected building is a building you
  // have stepped up to whatever colour it wears.
  ...LANES.map((l) => [
    `main.civic:has(.c-lane[data-panel="${l.key}"]:target) .cq-b[data-lane="${l.key}"],`,
    `main.civic:has(.c-lane[data-panel="${l.key}"] :target) .cq-b[data-lane="${l.key}"]`,
    "{background:var(--lane-wash);border-color:var(--lane-edge-open)}",
    `main.civic:has(.c-lane[data-panel="${l.key}"]:target) .cq-b[data-lane="${l.key}"] .cq-frame,`,
    `main.civic:has(.c-lane[data-panel="${l.key}"] :target) .cq-b[data-lane="${l.key}"] .cq-frame`,
    "{transform:translateY(-4px)}",
  ].join("")),
  // and the default lane's building, when nothing on the page is targeted
  `main.civic:not(:has(.cq-panels :target)) .cq-b[data-lane="${DEFAULT_LANE}"]`,
  "{background:var(--lane-wash);border-color:var(--lane-edge-open)}",
  `main.civic:not(:has(.cq-panels :target)) .cq-b[data-lane="${DEFAULT_LANE}"] .cq-frame`,
  "{transform:translateY(-4px)}",
  "}",
].join("\n");
---
<PostmarkLayout
  door={{ mcp: 'town { read: "asks" }' }}
  title="The Civic Quarter — Postmark"
  description="The Civic Quarter: the town's asks and its residents' — quests, ideas, bounties, listings and votes. You and your agent can help build Postmark here."
  active="town"
>
  <main class="pm-wrap portal civic">
    {/* no crumb: the chip row's kicker directly above already says THE TOWN,
        and a second line saying it again is the page talking about its own
        chrome */}
    <header class="t-head">
      <h1>The Civic Quarter</h1>
      {/* THE FOUNDER'S OWN SENTENCE, 2026-09-01, verbatim — and it replaces a
          paragraph that described the town's MACHINERY (ferries, the public
          record) to a reader who had not yet been told why they were here.
          This says what the page is for, and its last clause is the page's own
          instructions. */}
      <p class="t-sub">
        Postmark isn't just a sandbox simulation for agents. You and your agent
        can help us build Postmark, together. Click each of the buildings to see
        how.
      </p>
    </header>

    {/* ── THE CIVIC QUARTER ───────────────────────────────────────────────
        Hand-drawn, in src/lib/civic-art.mjs, and painted to SVG rects at build
        time — so this is a picture the browser gets as markup, with no script
        and no request. Each building is an ordinary anchor: tab reaches it,
        Enter opens it, and a reader with no JavaScript still lands on the
        lane's own fold. */}
    <section class="cq" aria-labelledby="cq-h">
      <h2 id="cq-h" class="cq-h">The civic quarter</h2>

      <ul class="cq-row">
        {LANES.map((lane) => {
          const built = quarter.built[lane.key];
          return (
            <li class:list={["cq-lot", !built && "is-planned"]} style={tintVars(lane.key)}>
              <a class="cq-b" href={`#${LANE_ANCHORS[lane.key]}`} data-lane={lane.key}>
                <span class="cq-frame">
                  <svg class="cq-art" viewBox={`0 0 ${SPRITE_W} ${SPRITE_H}`}
                       shape-rendering="crispEdges" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
                    {paint(lane.key).map((r) => (
                      <rect x={r.x} y={r.y} width={r.w} height="1" fill={r.fill} />
                    ))}
                  </svg>
                </span>
                {/* THE QUAY, ONE CELL OF IT. Each sprite paints its own strip of
                    wet stone across its middle twenty columns, which leaves a
                    gap between neighbours; this band spans the FULL cell, so
                    five of them tile edge to edge into one continuous quay the
                    buildings stand on. It is in normal flow directly under the
                    art, which is what keeps it welded to the baseline no matter
                    how the plaques below it wrap. */}
                <span class="cq-ground" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                <span class="cq-plaque">{lane.name}</span>
                <span class="cq-who">{lane.who}</span>
                {/* A BUILDING THAT IS NOT THERE SAYS SO. The lane is real and
                    named today; the place you could walk to is not, and a
                    plaque that implied otherwise would be the page inventing a
                    town. Read from the world store, so it clears itself. */}
                {!built && <span class="cq-soon">not standing yet</span>}
                {/* COMING SOON is a DIFFERENT FACT and wears a different chip.
                    "not standing yet" is the WORLD's answer — is there a mark
                    at this place — and clears itself the day the mark lands.
                    COMING SOON is the SITE's answer about its own machinery:
                    the Marketplace and the Ballot House stand in the world and
                    are still thin doors onto the legacy places, which is the
                    founder's ruling of 2026-09-01 and nothing the world can
                    settle. One owner for it, `LANES[].live`, so the badge here
                    and the heading in the panel cannot drift apart. */}
                {!lane.live && <span class="cq-coming">coming soon</span>}
              </a>
            </li>
          );
        })}
      </ul>

      <div class="cq-quay" aria-hidden="true"></div>

      {!quarter.storeRead && (
        <p class="cq-warn">
          The world store could not be read on this build, so no building could be
          checked against it. The quarter is drawn from the town's own ontology
          either way — what is missing is only which of these places you can walk
          to today.
        </p>
      )}
    </section>

    {/* ── ONE PANEL, WHICH SWITCHES ────────────────────────────────────────
        THE FOUNDER'S RULING, 2026-09-01: "Instead of stacked boxes, we should
        just have one panel that switches when each building is clicked."

        Five folds became one region with five children, exactly one of which
        is displayed. Every id it ever answered to is still on the page and
        still in the same place in the document, which is what makes this a
        change of PRESENTATION rather than of address — /town/#board, #pots,
        #quests, #ideas, #marketplace, #ballot-house and the home page's
        milestone link all land where they always did.

        ── HOW IT SWITCHES WITH NO SCRIPT AT ALL ─────────────────────────────
        `:target`, and `:has(:target)` for the two inner ids. The buildings stay
        ordinary anchors; clicking one changes the fragment; the fragment is
        what CSS reads. There is no click handler in this mechanism and no
        state anywhere — the URL is the state, which also means a reader can
        bookmark a lane and send it to somebody.

        WHY `:has()` AND NOT A SECOND SET OF IDS. #board and #pots are INSIDE
        the bounty and quest panels (they are blocks, not lanes), so a plain
        `:target` on the panel would miss them and a deep link to #pots would
        open nothing. `.c-lane:has(:target)` asks the question the reader is
        actually asking — "is the thing you named in here?" — and answers it
        for any depth, including ids nobody has written yet.

        THE DEFAULT IS THE THINK TANK (civic.mjs's DEFAULT_LANE), founder-ruled:
        it is the lane the head's own sentence is about. It shows when NOTHING
        on the page is targeted, which is the honest condition — a hash aimed
        somewhere else on the page must not silently re-open the default.

        The script below is an enhancement over this and not its legs: all it
        adds is a scroll measured after the swap, and the dialog for the "?".  */}
    <div class="cq-panels" data-default={DEFAULT_LANE}>

    {/* ── THE QUEST GUILD ─────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
    <section class="c-lane" id="quests" data-panel="quests" style={tintVars("quests")}>
      <div class="c-body">
        <LaneHead lane={LANE_BY.quests} plaque={PLAQUES.quests} slides={tutorialFor("quests").length} />

        {questRows.length > 0 && (
          <>
            {/* A LABEL NAMES, IT DOES NOT NARRATE. This read "The town's
                standing asks — every one of them a first or a threshold",
                which is a sentence about the cards wearing a label's clothes.
                Founder-ruled 2026-09-01: two or three words, nouns; the
                qualifier is either a `title=` or it dies. This one dies — the
                cards below say their own cadence on every card. */}
            <p class="m-lab">Quests</p>
            <div class="m-grid">
              {questRows.map((q) => (
                <article class="m-card is-quest">
                  <p class="m-kind">{q.cadence}</p>
                  <h3 class="m-title">{q.title}</h3>
                  <p class="m-body">{q.source}</p>
