                    a funding pot, and no transfer obligation. Rendering them
                    here as "backed" implied backers were paying, which misled.
                    The World shows a mark's stakes where every mark's stakes
                    live; this page shows the ask and the reward. The one bar
                    that STAYS is the civic threshold — a stake target the
                    town voted (board.mjs: civic-only by ruling); that bar is
                    law rendering, not backer display. */}
                {n.threshold && (
                  <div class="m-bar-wrap">
                    <div class="m-bar" role="img" aria-label={`${n.backed} of ${n.threshold} stamps staked`}>
                      <i style={`width:${Math.max(2, pct(n.progress))}%`}></i>
                    </div>
                    <p class="m-barlab"><b>{n.backed}</b> of {n.threshold} staked{n.backed > n.threshold && <span class="m-dim"> · over-subscribed</span>}</p>
                  </div>
                )}
                {/* THE STAKE, SHOWN AS A NUMBER AND NEVER AS "BACKED BY".
                    The founder's 2026-08-30 ruling stands and is the reason
                    this is one line rather than a bar: stakes on a bounty are
                    VISIBILITY, not funding, and no stamp here is owed to
                    anybody. So the page says how many stamps are on it and how
                    many houses put them there, and says nothing about who is
                    paying for what. Same ledger the ideas are ordered by. */}
                <p class="m-stake">
                  {stakeOf(n.id, stakes).staked == null
                    ? <span class="m-dim">uncounted — the escrow ledger could not be read on this build</span>
                    : <>
                        <b class="m-stamp">{stakeOf(n.id, stakes).staked}<span class="m-u">✦</span></b>
                        <span class="m-dim"> staked on it · {stakeOf(n.id, stakes).households} {stakeOf(n.id, stakes).households === 1 ? "household" : "households"}</span>
                      </>}
                </p>
                {/* THE DATE LEFT THE FOOT. The board is ordered by stake, so a
                    date orders nothing the reader can see; one chip on one calm
                    line is the whole footer now. */}
                <p class="m-foot">
                  <span class="m-chip is-stamp">{n.reward}<span class="m-u">✦</span> reward</span>
                </p>
              </article>
            ))}
          </div>
        )}

        {doneNotices.length > 0 && (
          <>
            <p class="m-lab">Done</p>
            <div class="m-grid m-done">
              {doneNotices.map((n) => (
                <article class="m-card is-done">
                  {/* "· done" LEFT EVERY CARD when the pile grew a label. It
                      was said once per card under no heading at all; it is said
                      once, above them, now. */}
                  <p class="m-kind">{n.poster}</p>
                  <h3 class="m-title">{n.ask}</h3>
                  <p class="m-foot"><span class="m-chip is-stamp">{n.reward}<span class="m-u">✦</span> reward</span></p>
                </article>
              ))}
            </div>
          </>
        )}

        {board.malformed.length > 0 && (
          <div class="m-none is-warn">
            <b>{board.malformed.length} notice{board.malformed.length === 1 ? "" : "s"} could not be read.</b>
            <span>Dropped rather than rendered half-built, and named rather than swallowed. An ask is one claim of at most {ASK_MAX} characters, a reward is a whole number of stamps, a status is <code>open</code> or <code>done</code>.</span>
            <ul>{board.malformed.map((m) => <li><code>{m.id}</code> — {m.reason}</li>)}</ul>
          </div>
        )}

        {/* THE WEIGHT PARAGRAPH IS GONE, founder-ruled 2026-08-31: "confusing
            for readers learning about this." It explained the breadth bonus —
            escrow plus k per distinct staking household — to a reader who had
            come to look at what the town is asking for, and the board does not
            render weight anywhere, so it was machinery with nothing on this
            page that shows it.

            CHECKED BEFORE REMOVING, per his instruction: /stamps/#staking
            already teaches it, and more precisely than this did — it quotes
            ECONOMY-DIALS.json § read_side.weight verbatim ("mark weight = sum
            of open escrows + k x unique staking households OTHER THAN the
            mark's own"), which this paraphrase silently dropped the exclusion
            from. It also carries "Posting a notice of your own" whole. The
            pointer's destination is intact; only the doorway from this lane is
            not, and that is the ruling. */}
      </div>

      {/* ── THE BOARD'S DASHBOARD, UNDER THE STATE ───────────────────────
          Founder-ruled 2026-09-01, the Guild's standings as the model. What is
          relevant to THIS lane is the shape of the asking: how many are open,
          how many are answered, what is on the line, which asks the town is
          looking hardest at, and who does the asking.

          THE POSTERS ARE RANKED BY NOTICES, NOT BY STAMPS, and that is the
          2026-08-30 ruling holding: a bounty's stake is visibility and not
          funding, so a poster leaderboard denominated in money would say the
          opposite of the law two blocks up this same panel. */}
      {/* "The board, counted" → "Counted", for the Think Tank's reason. */}
      <p class="m-lab">Counted</p>
      <ul class="d-counts">
        <li><b>{bountyBoard.open}</b> <span>open</span></li>
        <li><b>{bountyBoard.done}</b> <span>answered</span></li>
        <li>
          <b class="m-stamp">{bountyBoard.staked == null ? "—" : <>{bountyBoard.staked}<span class="m-u">✦</span></>}</b>
          <span>{bountyBoard.staked == null ? "uncounted" : "on the line"}</span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      {/* THE RANKING RENDERS ONLY WHEN THERE IS ONE TO MAKE. With a single
          staked notice on the board this listed that notice again, three
          inches under its own card — a leaderboard of one, which is not a
          leaderboard, it is a repetition. Read on the built page (2026-09-01)
          and cut there. At two it starts saying something the cards above do
          not, because the cards are already in this order and a reader who
          scrolled past them can see the head of it here. */}
      {bountyBoard.mostStaked.length > 1 && (
        <ol class="q-stand d-stand">
          {bountyBoard.mostStaked.map((n, i) => (
            <li class="q-row d-row">
              <span class="q-rank">{i + 1}</span>
              <span class="q-who d-ask">{n.ask}</span>
              <span class="q-pair"><span class="q-k">by</span> {n.poster}</span>
              <span class="q-all">{n.staked}<span class="q-u">✦</span></span>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ol>
      )}

      {bountyBoard.posters.length > 0 && (
        <p class="m-note">
          {/* THE NUMBER CARRIES ITS UNIT. This read "wright 1", which a reader
              parses as a name beside an unexplained figure — is that stamps,
              answers, a rank? It is asks, and now it says so. */}
          Who is asking:{" "}
          {bountyBoard.posters.map((p, i) => (
            <>{i > 0 && " · "}<span class="m-patron">{p.poster} <span class="m-dim">{p.notices} {p.notices === 1 ? "ask" : "asks"}</span></span></>
          ))}
        </p>
      )}
      {!bountyBoard.counted && (
        <p class="m-note m-dim">
          The escrow ledger could not be read on this build, so the
          <span class="m-stamp"><span class="m-u">✦</span></span> figures above
          are uncounted rather than zero — a build-input problem, not a board
          nobody has staked.
        </p>
      )}

      {/* one as-of, at the foot of the panel — see the Guild's */}
      {worldAsOf && <p class="c-asof">{worldAsOf}</p>}
      </div>
      <LaneTutorial lane={LANE_BY.bounties} slides={tutorialFor("bounties")} />
    </section>

    {/* ── THE MARKETPLACE ─────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
    <section class="c-lane" id="marketplace" data-panel="listings" style={tintVars("listings")}>
      <div class="c-body">
        <LaneHead lane={LANE_BY.listings} plaque={PLAQUES.listings} slides={tutorialFor("listings").length} />

        {/* THE MARKETPLACE'S PLAQUE IS THE PANEL'S TITLE NOW. It used to render
            twice on this lane — as a typed `c-law` at the top AND as
            `placeMarks[LANE_PLACES.listings]` in the `c-soon` block at the
            bottom, which is two readings of two different sentences about the
            same building. The mark's body is the heading; the block below keeps
            only the thing the mark does NOT say, which is what a reader should
            do today. */}

        {/* A SUMMARY AND A DOOR, NOT A SECOND PRICE BOARD. There is no listing
            class yet: the board is hand-set markdown kept by the office on its
            rounds. Re-rendering its rows here would be a second copy of a
            document whose whole first paragraph is about which copy is right,
            so this counts them and points at the one that is. */}
        {market.read ? (
          <>
            <ul class="mk-tally">
              <li><b>{market.asks}</b> <span>{market.asks === 1 ? "ask" : "asks"} — <em>I have X, asking N</em></span></li>
              <li><b>{market.wants}</b> <span>{market.wants === 1 ? "want" : "wants"} — <em>I want X, offering N</em></span></li>
              <li><b>{market.archived}</b> <span>filled or withdrawn</span></li>
            </ul>
            {/* ONE LINE AND THE DOOR, founder-ruled 2026-09-01, and it takes
                the place of TWO blocks: a five-fact paragraph (where every row
                is, how to list, how to want, that rows land on crossings, and
                that settlement is a `pays:` line — the last of which is a door
                verb on a human page) AND the dashed box under it repeating that
                the listing class has not landed.

                THE SHAPE IS THE BALLOT HOUSE'S, deliberately: one sentence
                saying where the thing lives, then a door. Two lanes that are
                both "not live yet, go through this door instead" should not be
                two different designs. */}
            <p class="m-note">
              Rows are hand-set by the office and a letter is what binds — every
              one of them is on <a href="/bulletin/#marketplace">the price board</a>.
            </p>
          </>
        ) : (
          <div class="m-none">
            <b>The price board could not be read.</b>
            <span>
              This tally counts the bulletin's own marketplace rows and this build
              did not find them in a shape it could read. The board itself is
              <a href="/bulletin/#marketplace"> on the bulletin</a>, which is the
              authority here regardless.
            </span>
          </div>
        )}

        {/* THE DOOR, gated on `LANES[].live` — still the one owner of "this
            lane's machinery has not landed", and still the gate, so the door
            goes the day the listing class does. `!quarter.built.listings` is
            NOT the gate: the building stands in the world today and the lane is
            still not live, and conflating those two facts is what would let
            this vanish while it was still true. */}
        {!LANE_BY.listings.live && (
          <p class="m-note">
            <a class="c-door" href="/mail/compose/?to=postmaster">Write the postmaster →</a>
          </p>
        )}
      </div>
    </section>

    {/* ── THE BALLOT HOUSE ────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
    <section class="c-lane" id="ballot-house" data-panel="votes" style={tintVars("votes")}>
      <div class="c-body">
        {/* THE BALLOT HOUSE'S PLAQUE IS FINALLY READ TOO — its mark is filed
            four levels down at `let-there-be-light/the-town-centre/the-keeping-
            works/the-ballot-house`, the deepest of the five, and the typed copy
            this lane carried went with the walk that could not reach it. */}
        <LaneHead lane={LANE_BY.votes} plaque={PLAQUES.votes} slides={tutorialFor("votes").length} />

        {/* THE TALLIES ARE NOT EMBEDDED, and that is a decision rather than an
            omission. /votes/ fetches the office's live ballot API on the client
            and re-renders itself between site builds; lifting that fetch onto
            the hub would put a second live poller on a page that already has
            one, and would make the hub's own freshness a question. The lane
            says what a vote IS and opens the door to the room that is already
            live. */}
        {/* ONE SENTENCE AND THE DOOR, founder-ruled 2026-08-31: the lane's
            description was "overtechnical and confusing" — minting, household
            headroom, liquid balance, cap bounces, the two cast doors and their
            three frontmatter keys, all before a reader had been told where the
            votes are.

            WHAT THE SENTENCE HAD TO GET RIGHT is where the ballots live, and
            it is read from the sources rather than composed: /votes/ is the
            Ballot Box and renders `GET /api/votes` client-side "so the page is
            current between site builds" (its own header), and the ballot
            house's world mark says one law decides every staked stamp there
            "whether cast at the counter or carried in by mail". So: the office
            answers, this build does not, and both doors land in the same room.

            NOTHING WAS LOST, checked rather than assumed: /votes/ itself
            carries the two doors under "How to cast a stake" and the clip /
            mint / final-for-the-window fine print verbatim, and the tri-law is
            in /stamps/#staking. Every fact struck here is one click away at the
            door this lane's whole job is to open. */}
        <p class="c-say">
          The town's open ballots live at <a href="/votes/">the Ballot Box</a>,
          tallied straight from the office rather than from this build — so what
          you see there is the vote as it stands right now, not as it stood when
          this page was made.
        </p>
        <p class="m-note">
          <a class="c-door" href="/votes/">Open the Ballot Box →</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    </div>{/* ── end of the panel ───────────────────────────────────────── */}

    {/* The switch itself — generated from LANES in the frontmatter above, and
        inline so its selectors reach the page unscoped. */}
    <style is:inline set:html={switchCss}></style>

    {/* ── THE RULES: STAMPS, THE MACHINERY UNDER ALL FIVE ──────────────────
        Not a sixth building. Stamps-the-currency is what every lane above runs
        on — a quest pays them, a bounty stakes them, a listing asks them, a
        ballot escrows them — so it is the ground under the quarter rather than
        a house in it. Everything /stamps/ carried is in here, moved whole. */}

    <p class="t-foot">
      The town itself — every letter, every ledger line, every resident's own
      files — lives in
      <a href="https://github.com/postmark-town/postmark" target="_blank" rel="noopener">the town repo</a>.
      {meeps.length > 0 && <> The <a href="/meeps/">{meeps.length} Meeps</a> who keep it running have rooms in there too.</>}
      {bulletin.length > 0 && <> What's pinned right now is on <a href="/bulletin/">the bulletin</a>.</>}
      {/* one expression, not two with a space between them: the space between
          adjacent {} expressions is dropped, and the shots caught it rendering
          as "86households have declared themselves" */}
      {declaredHouses > 0 && <> {`${declaredHouses} ${declaredHouses === 1 ? "household has" : "households have"}`} declared themselves.</>}
    </p>
  </main>
</PostmarkLayout>

<!-- THE PANEL'S SWITCH IS CSS AND IS NOT IN HERE.
     `:target` and `:has(:target)` do the swap — see the `<style is:inline>`
     below and the comment above the panel. That matters for what this script
     is allowed to be: not the mechanism, only two conveniences over it.

       1. THE SCROLL, measured after the swap. A fragment lands on a panel that
          was `display:none` a frame ago, so the browser's own jump can measure
          the pre-swap layout and stop somewhere else entirely. rAF is the fix,
          and it is the same fix the fold router used for the same reason.
       2. THE "?" DIALOG. A native <dialog> needs showModal() called, and the
          deck needs a step. Both are enhancement by definition — with the
          script off the "?" simply does nothing and the panel under it is the
          whole page, unchanged.

     WHAT LEFT WITH THE FOLDS: `reveal()` (there is nothing to open any more),
     the data-jump handler and its pushState (an in-page link is an ORDINARY
     ANCHOR now — changing the fragment is what switches the panel, so a
     preventDefault would have to reimplement the thing it was intercepting),
     and the a[data-lane] click handler (same reason: the anchor already does
     it). Every one of those was machinery for opening a <details>. -->
<script is:inline>
  (function () {
    var main = document.querySelector("main.civic");
    if (!main) return;

    function scrollToHash() {
      var id = decodeURIComponent((location.hash || "").slice(1));
      if (!id) return;
      var el = document.getElementById(id);
      if (!el) return;
      requestAnimationFrame(function () {
        el.scrollIntoView({ block: "start", behavior: "smooth" });
      });
    }
    // ARRIVAL ONLY (founder, 2026-09-01 night, hotfix w37.1): a deep link
    // still lands on its panel, but clicking a building no longer scrolls —
    // the panel switches in place (the CSS swap) and the page stays where
    // the reader put it. The hashchange listener that scrolled every click
    // is gone; the native jump already no-ops because the target is
    // display:none until :target applies.
    scrollToHash();

    // ── THE CLICK DOES NOT SCROLL (founder, 2026-09-02 — w37.2) ─────────
    // w37.1 removed the script's corrective scroll, but the BROWSER's own
    // anchor jump remained: the :target swap makes the panel visible in the
    // same style pass, so the native jump finds a box after all (the old
    // comment said exactly this and w37.1 mis-read it as a no-op). The
    // anchor's whole job here is the fragment — so do the fragment by hand
    // and pin the scroll in the same task: no painted movement. pushState
    // stays rejected (:target ignores it); location.hash keeps :target true.
    // Script off = ordinary anchors, with the browser's jump — the floor
    // stands, unmoved.
    main.addEventListener("click", function (e) {
      var a = e.target.closest && e.target.closest('a[href^="#"]');
      if (!a || !main.contains(a)) return;
      var id = decodeURIComponent((a.getAttribute("href") || "").slice(1));
      if (!id || !document.getElementById(id)) return;
      e.preventDefault();
      var x = window.scrollX, y = window.scrollY;
      location.hash = id;
      window.scrollTo(x, y);
    });

    // ── the "?" bubble ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
    // One handler for every deck. `step` shows exactly one slide and moves the
    // dots and the two buttons with it, so there is one place that knows which
    // slide is showing rather than three that can disagree.
    function step(dialog, to) {
      var slides = dialog.querySelectorAll(".c-tut-slide");
      var dots = dialog.querySelectorAll(".c-tut-dot");
      if (!slides.length) return;
      var at = Math.max(0, Math.min(slides.length - 1, to));
      for (var i = 0; i < slides.length; i++) {
        slides[i].hidden = i !== at;
        if (dots[i]) dots[i].className = "c-tut-dot" + (i === at ? " is-on" : "");
      }
      dialog.dataset.at = String(at);
      var prev = dialog.querySelector(".c-tut-prev");
      var next = dialog.querySelector(".c-tut-next");
      if (prev) prev.disabled = at === 0;
      if (next) next.disabled = at === slides.length - 1;
    }

    main.addEventListener("click", function (e) {
      var close = e.target.closest && e.target.closest(".c-tut-prev, .c-tut-next");
      if (close) {
        var deck = close.closest("dialog");
        if (!deck) return;
        step(deck, Number(deck.dataset.at || 0) + (close.classList.contains("c-tut-next") ? 1 : -1));
        return;
      }
      var ask = e.target.closest && e.target.closest("[data-tut]");
      if (!ask) return;
      var dialog = document.getElementById("tut-" + ask.getAttribute("data-tut"));
      // A browser with no <dialog> gets no modal and no error — the panel it
      // would have explained is right there, which is the fallback.
      if (!dialog || typeof dialog.showModal !== "function") return;
      step(dialog, 0);
      dialog.showModal();
    });

    // Arrow keys inside an open deck, because a slideshow that only takes
    // clicks is a slideshow a keyboard reader cannot walk.
    main.addEventListener("keydown", function (e) {
      var dialog = e.target.closest && e.target.closest("dialog.c-tut");
      if (!dialog || !dialog.open) return;
      if (e.key === "ArrowRight") step(dialog, Number(dialog.dataset.at || 0) + 1);
      if (e.key === "ArrowLeft") step(dialog, Number(dialog.dataset.at || 0) - 1);
    });
  })();
</script>

<style>
  /* ── the town's own head (unchanged from the bare read) ─────────────── */
  .civic { max-width: 62rem; }
  .t-head { text-align: center; margin: 0 0 2em; }
  .t-head h1 {
    margin: 0; font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 2.9rem);
    color: var(--pm-gold-bright); text-shadow: 0 0 34px rgba(232, 196, 139, 0.35);
  }
  .t-sub {
    max-width: 62ch; margin: 0.8em auto 0; color: #cdc2ab;
    font-size: 0.97rem; line-height: 1.65;
  }
  .t-sub a { color: var(--pm-gold); }

  .t-foot {
    max-width: 62ch; margin: 2.6em auto 0; text-align: center;
    font-size: 0.85rem; line-height: 1.65; color: rgba(205, 194, 171, 0.65);
  }
  .t-foot a { color: var(--pm-gold); }

  /* ── THE CIVIC QUARTER ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
     A warm accent on the site's own weather, not a theme takeover: the night
     is the town's night (--pm-night → --pm-harbor), the plaques are the same
     mono the chips wear, and nothing here invents a colour. */
  .cq {
    margin: 0 0 2.4rem;
    padding: 1.4rem 1rem 0;
    border: 1px solid rgba(232, 196, 139, 0.18);
    border-radius: 14px;
    background:
      radial-gradient(120% 90% at 50% 0%, rgba(232, 196, 139, 0.07), transparent 60%),
      linear-gradient(var(--pm-harbor, #0d1426), var(--pm-night, #070b15));
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  /* The bottom margin was the description paragraph's; with the description
     gone the heading has to hold that gap itself, or the buildings ride up
     into it. */
  .cq-h {
    margin: 0 0 1.4rem; text-align: center;
    font-family: var(--pm-mono); font-size: 0.72rem; letter-spacing: 0.18em;
    text-transform: uppercase; color: rgba(232, 196, 139, 0.75); font-weight: 400;
  }

  /* THE BUILDINGS STAND ON ONE LINE, and `flex-start` is what does it. Aligned
     to flex-END the row hangs each cell from its own bottom edge — and the
     cells are different heights, because three of the five carry a "not
     standing yet" chip and the descriptions wrap to different depths. The
     result was five buildings at five different altitudes on what is supposed
     to be one quay. Every sprite is the same height with its ground on the same
     row, so aligning the TOPS puts every building on the same ground. */
  .cq-row {
    list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0;
    display: flex; align-items: flex-start; justify-content: center;
    gap: 0; flex-wrap: wrap;
  }
  .cq-lot { flex: 0 1 auto; }

  /* A FIXED CELL WIDTH, so the five quay bands tile into one strip instead of
     each stopping at whatever width its own caption happened to need. */
  .cq-b {
    display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center;
    gap: 0; padding: 0.5rem 0 0.7rem; width: 8.6rem;
    text-decoration: none; color: inherit;
    border-radius: 10px; border: 1px solid transparent;
    transition: background 0.14s ease, border-color 0.14s ease;
  }
  .cq-frame { display: block; line-height: 0; }
  .cq-ground {
    display: block; width: 100%; height: 7px; margin-bottom: 0.45rem;
    background: linear-gradient(rgba(70, 85, 119, 0.85), rgba(47, 58, 86, 0.95));
    box-shadow: inset 0 1px 0 rgba(120, 140, 180, 0.35);
  }
  .cq-art {
    display: block; width: 88px; height: 88px;
    image-rendering: pixelated;
    filter: drop-shadow(0 0 12px rgba(232, 196, 139, 0.12));
    transition: filter 0.14s ease;
  }
  .cq-plaque {
    font-family: var(--pm-mono); font-size: 0.68rem; letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    color: var(--pm-gold); margin-top: 0.35rem; text-align: center;
  }
  .cq-who {
    font-size: 0.62rem; line-height: 1.4; text-align: center;
    color: rgba(205, 194, 171, 0.5); max-width: 13ch;
  }
  .cq-soon {
    font-family: var(--pm-mono); font-size: 0.55rem; letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase; color: rgba(205, 194, 171, 0.45);
    border: 1px dashed rgba(205, 194, 171, 0.28); border-radius: 999px;
    padding: 0.15em 0.55em; margin-top: 0.3rem;
  }

  /* A LANE THE TOWN HAS NAMED AND NOT BUILT reads as unlit rather than as
     missing — the building is drawn, dimmed, with its plaque intact. */
  .is-planned .cq-art { filter: saturate(0.4) brightness(0.62); }
  .is-planned .cq-plaque { color: rgba(232, 196, 139, 0.55); }

  /* THE BUILDING LIFTS, THE QUAY DOES NOT. Hovering the whole cell would carry
     the quay band up with it and tear a notch in the strip its neighbours are
     still holding level, so the transform lives on the art alone. */
  .cq-b:hover, .cq-b:focus-visible {
    background: rgba(232, 196, 139, 0.07);
    border-color: rgba(232, 196, 139, 0.3);
  }
  .cq-frame { transition: transform 0.14s ease; }
  .cq-b:hover .cq-frame, .cq-b:focus-visible .cq-frame { transform: translateY(-4px); }
  .cq-b:hover .cq-art, .cq-b:focus-visible .cq-art {
    filter: drop-shadow(0 0 16px rgba(232, 196, 139, 0.4));
  }
  .cq-b:hover .cq-plaque, .cq-b:focus-visible .cq-plaque { color: var(--pm-gold-bright); }
  .cq-b:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--pm-gold); outline-offset: 2px; }

  /* the wet stone the whole quarter stands on */
  .cq-quay {
    height: 14px; margin: 0 -1rem;
    background:
      linear-gradient(rgba(70, 85, 119, 0.55), rgba(47, 58, 86, 0.9));
    border-top: 2px solid rgba(70, 85, 119, 0.7);
  }
  .cq-warn {
    margin: 0.9rem 0 0; padding: 0 0 1rem;
    font-size: 0.78rem; line-height: 1.6; color: rgba(205, 194, 171, 0.7);
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .cq-b, .cq-frame { transition: none; }
    .cq-b:hover .cq-frame, .cq-b:focus-visible .cq-frame { transform: none; }
  }

  /* ── the lanes ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  /* EACH PANEL WEARS ITS BUILDING'S COLOUR, founder-ruled 2026-08-31: "colour
     the panels similarly to their pixel art buildings." The four `--lane-*`
     properties are set inline per lane from civic-art.mjs's own ACCENTS table
     — the very table `paint()` inks the sprite from — so a lane's panel and its
     building can never disagree about what colour that lane is. NO HEX IS
     TYPED HERE, and the falsifier in test/civic.test.mjs is the thing that
     keeps it that way; the moment one is, the panels have a second palette.

     The fallbacks are the gold the lanes wore before today. They are not
     decoration: a panel rendered outside this page, or one whose lane key ever
     stops matching a sprite, falls back to the site's own chrome instead of to
     `transparent`, which would read as a missing border rather than as a
     styling gap. */
  .c-lane {
    border: 1px solid var(--lane-edge, rgba(232, 196, 139, 0.18));
    border-radius: 12px;
    margin: 0;
    background: rgba(14, 22, 42, 0.4);
    /* the sticky rail must not sit on top of the panel a fragment just opened */
    scroll-margin-top: 4.5rem;
  }
  /* THE WASH RIDES THE HEAD, not the body. A panel whose whole body took its
     accent would stop being the town's night and start being its own page —
     the building is what should carry the colour, and the panel only has to
     agree with it. With one panel showing at a time this matters MORE than it
     did with five folds: a flooded panel would repaint the whole page every
     time a reader clicked a different building. */
  .c-lane > .c-body { border-radius: 12px; }
  .c-lane > .c-body > .c-top {
    background: var(--lane-wash, transparent);
    border-radius: 11px 11px 0 0;
    margin: 0 -1.5rem 2.6rem; padding: 1.5rem 1.5rem 1.6rem;
  }
  .cq-panels { margin: 0 0 0.75rem; }

  /* ── THE SCALE, AND IT IS THE WHOLE OF RULE 5 ──────────────────────────────
     THE FOUNDER, 2026-09-01: "The spacing scale between blocks roughly doubles
     (panel head → state → dashboard); card padding up; meters, chips and
     footers on one calm line each … Type scale: plaque big (as now), labels
     small caps, everything else one body size — no third and fourth sizes."

     THREE TIERS, DECLARED ONCE, and every rule below reads them:

       --civic-plaque  the panel's title — the world's own sentence (LaneHead)
       --civic-label   small caps: block labels, kind lines, ranks, chips, as-of
       --civic-body    everything else, including card titles

     A CARD TITLE AT BODY SIZE is the part that looks wrong in a diff and right
     on the page: it is told apart by COLOUR (gold-bright on cream) rather than
     by a fourth size, which is what "no third and fourth sizes" costs and buys.
     Before this the panel rendered .66 / .70 / .72 / .78 / .80 / .82 / .84 /
     .86 / .92 / 1 / 1.5rem — eleven sizes on one screen, which is the "hard on
     the eyes" the founder read.

     THE SPACING IS MARGIN-COLLAPSED ON PURPOSE. `.m-lab` carries the big top
     margin and the head carries the big bottom one; adjacent siblings collapse
     to the larger of the two, so the first label after the head gets one gap
     and not two, with no `:first-child` special case to forget. */
  main.civic {
    --civic-body: .9rem;
    --civic-label: .7rem;
    /* the gap BETWEEN blocks — head → state → dashboard */
    --civic-block: 2.8rem;
  }
  .c-body { padding: 0 1.5rem 2.2rem; font-size: var(--civic-body); }

  /* THE ONE AS-OF LINE, at the foot of the panel. It replaced four `n-asof`
     spans riding four block labels, each naming the same build — the founder's
     "the as-of stamps stay but move to ONE small line per panel". It is a
     label, not prose, so it wears the label tier. */
  .c-asof {
    margin: var(--civic-block) 0 0;
    font-family: var(--pm-mono); font-size: var(--civic-label);
    letter-spacing: .1em; color: rgba(205, 194, 171, .45);
  }

  /* `.c-law` IS GONE, and so is its `cite`. The world's sentence is the
     panel's HEADING now (LaneHead.astro) — founder-ruled 2026-09-01, both
     halves: "The marks that we drafted up should be really big font (like the
     title of that panel)" and "Don't include distracting text like 'the
     world's own words, at the-town/quest'." A pull-quote style with nothing
     left to quote is how the block would grow a second sentence back. */

  /* 74ch, not 60: at 60 this paragraph wrapped into a narrow column beside a
     law quote and a note that both run the lane's full width, and the ragged
     right edge read as a layout fault rather than as a measure. */
  .c-say { font-size: var(--civic-body); line-height: 1.7; max-width: 74ch; margin: 0; }
  /* `.c-soon` IS GONE with the dashed box it drew. The Marketplace's "the
     listing class has not landed" note was a paragraph inside a border saying
     what its own door already says, and the founder's rule 2 collapsed the two
     into one line plus that door. The Ballot House's shape — one sentence, then
     `.c-door` — is what both not-live lanes wear now, so there is one design
     for one situation rather than two. */
  .c-door {
    display: inline-block;
    font-family: var(--pm-mono); font-size: var(--civic-label); letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    padding: 0.45em 1em; border-radius: 999px;
    border: 1px solid rgba(232, 196, 139, 0.4); color: var(--pm-gold-bright);
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .c-door:hover { background: rgba(232, 196, 139, 0.1); }

  /* ── the Quest Guild's standings ───────────────────────────────────── */
  .q-stand { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; }
  .q-row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 2em minmax(8em, 1fr) auto auto 4em;
    align-items: baseline; gap: 0.9em;
    padding: 0.55em 0; font-size: var(--civic-body);
    border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(232, 196, 139, 0.08);
  }
  .q-rank { font-family: var(--pm-mono); font-size: var(--civic-label); color: rgba(205, 194, 171, 0.5); }
  .q-who { color: var(--pm-gold); text-decoration: none; }
  .q-who:hover { color: var(--pm-gold-bright); text-decoration: underline; }
  .q-pair { color: rgba(205, 194, 171, 0.8); white-space: nowrap; }
  .q-k {
    font-family: var(--pm-mono); font-size: var(--civic-label); letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase; color: rgba(205, 194, 171, 0.45);
  }
  .q-all { font-family: var(--pm-mono); color: var(--pm-stamp-bright); text-align: right; }
  .q-u { opacity: 0.7; }

  /* ── the Guild's quest cards ───────────────────────────────────────────
     They wear the board's own card family (.m-card) on purpose: a quest and a
     bounty are the same shape of thing seen from the two ends of the asks
     matrix — the town asking, and a resident asking — so they should look like
     siblings rather than like two designs. The bar is the daily completion
     count, in stamp violet because what it counts is minting. */
  .m-card.is-quest { border-color: rgba(var(--pm-stamp-rgb), 0.28); }
  /* A DEAD RULE, FOUND BY THE ONE-PURPLE FALSIFIER (2026-09-01). This read
     `rgba(var(--pm-stamp-bright, #d8c7ef), 0.75)`, which cannot work: the
     token IS a hex, so the declaration expanded to `rgba(#d8c7ef, 0.75)` —
     invalid, dropped by every browser, and the quest card's kind line has
     therefore been wearing the inherited grey since the day it was written.
     Nobody could see it because a dropped declaration looks exactly like a
     declaration nobody wrote.

     `--pm-stamp-rgb` is the channel list postmark.css publishes for precisely
     this ("so a surface can wash, tint or hairline in the family without
     inventing a hex"), and the fallback hex goes with the bug — a fallback
     that is a typed copy of a token is the second palette all over again. */
  .m-card.is-quest .m-kind { color: rgba(var(--pm-stamp-rgb), 0.75); }
  .m-card.is-quest .m-bar { background: rgba(var(--pm-stamp-rgb), 0.14); }
  .m-card.is-quest .m-bar i {
    background: linear-gradient(90deg, rgba(var(--pm-stamp-dark-rgb), 0.75), var(--pm-stamp));
  }
  .m-card.is-quest .m-chip {
    border-color: rgba(var(--pm-stamp-rgb), 0.4);
    color: var(--pm-stamp-bright);
  }

  /* ── COMING SOON, on the building ──────────────────────────────────────
     A DIFFERENT CHIP FROM "not standing yet", deliberately: that one is the
     world's answer and clears itself; this one is the site's own fact about
     its machinery and clears when `LANES[].live` does. Filled rather than
     outlined, so the two never read as the same badge at a glance. */
  .cq-coming {
    font-family: var(--pm-mono); font-size: 0.5rem; letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--pm-night);
    background: rgba(232, 196, 139, 0.78); border-radius: 999px;
    padding: 0.2em 0.6em; margin-top: 0.3rem; white-space: nowrap;
  }

  /* ── the dashboards ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     The Guild's standings are the model, so the standing itself reuses
     `.q-stand`/`.q-row` outright rather than growing a second leaderboard
     that can drift from it. What is new is the counts strip above it, which
     wears the Marketplace tally's shape for the same reason — the site
     already has one way to show "N of a thing". */
  /* ONE ROW OF NUMBER+WORD PAIRS WITH REAL GAPS, founder-ruled 2026-09-01. The
     gap doubled (1.6rem → 3rem) and the figure came DOWN from 1.5rem to the
     body size: at 1.5rem the four numbers were a second headline competing with
     the plaque, which is the "third and fourth sizes" the same rule forbids.
     A pair reads as a pair when the two halves are the same size and only the
     colour differs. */
  .d-counts {
    list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0;
    display: flex; gap: 1rem 3rem; flex-wrap: wrap;
  }
  .d-counts li { display: flex; align-items: baseline; gap: 0.5em; }
  .d-counts b {
    font-family: var(--pm-mono); font-size: var(--civic-body); font-weight: 400;
    color: var(--pm-gold-bright);
  }
  /* THE SAME SPECIFICITY TRAP, ONE LEVEL UP, and again the rendered page is
     what found it: `.d-counts b` (0,1,1) beats `.m-stamp` (0,1,0), so a ✦
     figure in a counts line was painted the block's gold no matter what class
     it wore. A stamp figure has to out-specify the block it sits in. */
  .d-counts b.m-stamp { color: var(--pm-stamp-bright); }
  /* `> li >`, NOT a descendant selector, and the rendered page is what caught
     it: as `.d-counts span` this also matched the `.m-u` INSIDE the figure —
     `.d-counts span` (0,1,1) beats `.m-u` (0,1,0) — so the ✦ in every counts
     line was painted the label's cream while the number beside it was purple.
     One stamp glyph, two colours, half a rem apart, and nothing in the source
     said so: the rule that broke the stamp law was a rule about labels. */
  .d-counts > li > span { font-size: var(--civic-body); color: rgba(205, 194, 171, 0.75); line-height: 1.5; }
  .d-counts .m-stamp .m-u { color: inherit; }
  .d-stand { margin-top: 1.6rem; }
  .d-row { grid-template-columns: 2em minmax(8em, 1fr) auto 5em; }
  .d-ask { color: rgba(244, 233, 215, 0.9); }

  /* ── ONE PURPLE, EVERYWHERE A ✦ IS RENDERED ────────────────────────────
     THE LAW, verbatim from src/styles/postmark.css: "stamps are purple (law,
     Keemin 2026-07-29) — every stamp-touching surface (mint bar, balances,
     backing, ✦-weight) uses THIS family, no per-page hex".

     THIS LANE WAS BREAKING IT IN TWO PLACES and had been since the board
     moved here: the bounty reward chip and the pot's staked chip both rendered
     a ✦ inside a plain `.m-chip`, which is gold. Nothing invented a hex — the
     stamps simply wore the page's chrome colour instead of their own, which
     is the same failure one step quieter. `is-stamp` is the chip in the stamp
     family, read from the tokens, and every ✦ on this page now sits inside
     something that carries it. */
  .m-stamp { color: var(--pm-stamp-bright); font-weight: 400; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  .m-u { opacity: 0.7; margin-left: 0.08em; }
  .m-stake { font-size: var(--civic-body); line-height: 1.6; margin: 0.15rem 0 0; }
  /* WHERE AN IDEA STANDS, when that is not the Tank — one small line, dim,
     because it is a fact about the mark and not the ask itself. */
  .m-stand { font-size: var(--civic-body); line-height: 1.6; margin: 0; opacity: .55; }
  .m-chip.is-stamp {
    border-color: rgba(var(--pm-stamp-rgb), 0.4);
    color: var(--pm-stamp-bright);
  }
  .m-card.is-idea { border-color: rgba(var(--pm-stamp-rgb), 0.22); }

  /* ── the Marketplace's tally ─────────────────────────────────────────
     The counts dashboard's twin, and it takes the same shape for the same
     reason: one row of number+word pairs, real gaps, one body size. */
  .mk-tally { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; display: flex; gap: 1rem 3rem; flex-wrap: wrap; }
  .mk-tally li { display: flex; align-items: baseline; gap: 0.5em; }
  .mk-tally b { font-family: var(--pm-mono); font-size: var(--civic-body); color: var(--pm-gold-bright); font-weight: 400; }
  .mk-tally span { font-size: var(--civic-body); color: rgba(205, 194, 171, 0.75); line-height: 1.5; }
  .mk-tally em { font-style: italic; opacity: 0.75; }

  @media (max-width: 720px) {
    /* five cells will not fit a phone, so the quarter wraps to two ranks. The
       captions go: at this width the plaque is the label, and the lane's own
       fold repeats the description one screen further down anyway. */
    .cq-art { width: 60px; height: 60px; }
    /* wide enough that "not standing yet" stays on one line — a chip broken
       across two reads as a layout fault rather than as a label */
    .cq-b { width: 6.4rem; }
    .cq-who { display: none; }
    .cq-plaque { font-size: 0.6rem; }
    .cq-soon { font-size: 0.5rem; letter-spacing: 0.04em; white-space: nowrap; }
    .q-row { grid-template-columns: 1.6em 1fr auto; row-gap: 0.15em; }
    /* `.q-pair { font-size: .72rem }` LEFT THIS BREAKPOINT. It was a third body
       size that only existed under 720px — invisible to a desktop read and
       invisible to a source read that did not think to look inside a media
       query, which is exactly where a scale quietly grows a fourth tier. The
       row already drops two columns at this width; it does not also need
       smaller type. */
  }

  .portal { max-width: 62rem; margin: 0 auto; }

  /* ── the head: one breath, then the market ─────────────────────────── */
  .p-head { margin-bottom: 1.6rem; }
  .p-crumb { font-size: .7rem; letter-spacing: .18em; opacity: .6; margin: 0 0 .3rem; }
  .portal h1 { margin: 0 0 .6rem; }
  .p-glyph { color: var(--pm-gold); }
  .p-folk {
    margin: 0; font-size: 1.02rem; line-height: 1.6; font-style: italic;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--pm-gold); padding: .55rem 1rem;
    background: rgba(232, 196, 139, .05);
  }
  .p-give { margin: .9rem 0 0; font-size: .9rem; line-height: 1.6; max-width: 44rem; opacity: .85; }
  .p-primer { margin-top: .9rem; }
  .p-primer > summary { list-style: none; cursor: pointer; display: inline-block; }
  .p-primer > summary::-webkit-details-marker { display: none; }
  .p-primer-lab {
    font-family: var(--pm-mono); font-size: .7rem; letter-spacing: .1em; text-transform: uppercase;
    border: 1px solid rgba(232, 196, 139, .3); border-radius: 999px; padding: .35em .9em;
    color: rgba(232, 196, 139, .85);
  }
  .p-primer[open] .p-primer-lab { background: rgba(232, 196, 139, .1); color: var(--pm-gold-bright); }
  .p-primer-lab::after { content: " +"; opacity: .6; }
  .p-primer[open] .p-primer-lab::after { content: " −"; }
  .p-five { counter-reset: five; list-style: none; margin: 1rem 0 0; padding: 0; max-width: 44rem; }
  .p-five li { counter-increment: five; position: relative; padding: .4rem 0 .4rem 2.2rem; line-height: 1.6; font-size: .92rem; }
  .p-five li::before { content: counter(five); position: absolute; left: 0; top: .4rem; font-family: var(--pm-mono); color: var(--pm-gold); opacity: .8; }
  .p-five b { color: var(--pm-gold-bright); font-weight: 400; }

  /* ── the stalls ────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .p-tabs {
    position: sticky; top: 0; z-index: 5;
    display: flex; gap: .4rem; padding: .7rem 0;
    background: linear-gradient(var(--pm-night, #0b1020) 72%, transparent);
    border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(232, 196, 139, .18); margin-bottom: 1.6rem;
  }
  .p-tab {
    font-family: var(--pm-mono); font-size: .74rem; letter-spacing: .1em; text-transform: uppercase;
    padding: .5em 1.1em; border-radius: 999px; cursor: pointer;
    border: 1px solid rgba(232, 196, 139, .25);
    background: transparent; color: rgba(232, 196, 139, .75);
  }
  .p-tab:hover { color: var(--pm-gold-bright); background: rgba(232, 196, 139, .07); }
  .p-tab.is-on { background: var(--pm-gold); color: var(--pm-night, #0b1020); border-color: var(--pm-gold); }

  /* ── the market ────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .m-block + .m-block { margin-top: var(--civic-block); }
  /* A LABEL NAMES. Two or three words, small caps, and the big gap lives HERE
     rather than on the blocks themselves — a label is always the top of a
     block, so one margin does the whole scale and collapses against whatever
     was above it. `n-asof` went with the four per-block stamps it rode. */
  .m-lab {
    font-size: var(--civic-label); letter-spacing: .16em; text-transform: uppercase; opacity: .6;
    border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(232, 196, 139, .2); padding-bottom: .5rem;
    margin: var(--civic-block) 0 1.1rem;
  }
  /* ── AND NOTHING SCROLLS SIDEWAYS HERE EITHER ──────────────────────────────
     `min(19rem, 100%)`, not `19rem`. REPRODUCED at 390px before it was touched,
     on the base build as well as this branch: `/town/#quests` and `/town/#pots`
     made the DOCUMENT 448px wide in a 390px window (463 on this branch, whose
     roomier chip padding widened the same unbreakable box).

     WHY NOBODY SAW IT, and this is the finding worth more than the fix: the
     rendered 390px check loads `/town/` with no fragment, where the panel that
     overflows is `display: none`. The one-panel switch that landed on 09-01
     took four fifths of this page out of every rendered measurement that does
     not name a fragment. An overflow check on a page with a switch has to walk
     the switch.

     THE CAUSE. `minmax(19rem, 1fr)` floors the track at 304px, and the card
     inside it cannot shrink below its own min-content — which the Budding
     friendship quest's reward chip sets, because `.m-chip` was `white-space:
     nowrap` and that reward is a 50-character sentence ("5 stamps to each of
     you at 5 each way; 10 each at 10"). `min(19rem, 100%)` lets the track fall
     to the container, `min-width: 0` lets the card follow it, and the chip
     rule below stops the sentence being unbreakable in the first place. */
  .m-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(min(19rem, 100%), 1fr)); gap: 1.1rem; }
  .m-grid + .m-grid { margin-top: 1.1rem; }
  /* CARD PADDING UP, founder-ruled — and the inner gap with it, because a card
     with room around it and none inside it just moves the crowding. */
  .m-card {
    display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: .6rem; min-width: 0;
    padding: 1.2rem 1.3rem 1.25rem;
    border: 1px solid rgba(232, 196, 139, .16); border-radius: .55rem;
    background: rgba(232, 196, 139, .028);
  }
  .m-card.is-civic { border-left: 3px solid var(--pm-gold); }
  .m-card.is-done { opacity: .55; }
  .m-card.is-done .m-title { text-decoration: line-through; }
  .m-card.is-settled { opacity: .7; }
  .m-kind { font-family: var(--pm-mono); font-size: var(--civic-label); letter-spacing: .12em; text-transform: uppercase; opacity: .62; margin: 0; }
  /* THE CARD TITLE IS TOLD APART BY COLOUR, NOT BY SIZE — see § THE SCALE. */
  .m-title { font-size: var(--civic-body); line-height: 1.45; margin: 0; color: var(--pm-gold-bright); font-weight: 400; }
  .m-body { font-size: var(--civic-body); line-height: 1.6; opacity: .78; margin: 0; }
  .m-money { font-size: var(--civic-body); margin: 0; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  /* ONE CALM LINE EACH. A meter is its bar and its one label, with the label
     on the same baseline rhythm as everything else rather than a size of its
     own. */
  .m-bar-wrap { margin: 0; }
  .m-bar { height: .4rem; border-radius: 999px; background: rgba(128,128,128,.25); overflow: hidden; }
  .m-bar:not(.is-pot-bar) i { display: block; height: 100%; background: var(--pm-gold); border-radius: 999px; }
  .m-bar.is-pot-bar i { display: block; height: 100%; border-radius: 999px; }
  .m-barlab { font-size: var(--civic-body); margin: .5rem 0 0; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  .m-what { font-size: var(--civic-body); line-height: 1.6; opacity: .82; margin: 0; }
  .m-holo { padding: 0 .3em; border-radius: 999px; }
  .m-roll { font-size: var(--civic-body); margin: 0; line-height: 1.7; }
  .m-roll a { color: inherit; }
  .m-gift { font-style: italic; opacity: .8; }
  .m-patron { white-space: nowrap; }
  .m-dim { opacity: .6; }
  .m-foot { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: center; gap: .7rem; margin: .1rem 0 0; font-size: var(--civic-body); }
  /* A CHIP IS A LABEL and wears the label tier — small caps mono, not a fourth
     body size.

     AND IT WRAPS UNLESS IT IS A FIGURE. `white-space: nowrap` was on every
     chip, and it is right for exactly one of them: a stamp figure — "12✦
     reward", "4✦ staked" — must never break between the number and its unit.
     The QUEST chip carries the registry's own reward SENTENCE, and a sentence
